WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:01.787
- What, you don't think
I know what I'm doing?

00:00:01.787 --> 00:00:04.610
- No, no, no, I'm not
saying that, I'm just...

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.591
- I'm saying both of you
don't know what you're doing.

00:00:06.591 --> 00:00:10.000
(whimsical music)

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.580
- So I've actually never
seen a Dhar Mann video before

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.880
or Dhar Mhan or Dhar Man?

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.260
Apologies to Mr. Mann.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.750
I've never seen one of your videos.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.110
A couple of people asked
me to react to this

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.120
and based on the thumbnail,
this looks pretty terrible.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:23.953
So let's react

00:00:23.953 --> 00:00:27.150
to "Prosecutor Sends
Innocent Black Man to Jail".

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.220
- So you see, your honor,
it's without question

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.890
that this defendant did these crimes.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:35.100
- No. That's impossible.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.660
- Okay, who's the prosecutor
arguing in front of?

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.860
Where's the jury in this?

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.600
And why is the defendant

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.410
being allowed to speak at this point?

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.100
What part of the trial are we in?

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.320
This doesn't make any
sense right off the bat.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.560
- I wasn't in Philly when it happened.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.530
I was in LA at a Dodgers game!

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:52.770
- He's lying.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.530
- I mean, you know,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:57.330
you have a lawyer to talk
to the judge and the jury.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.450
You're not really allowed to talk

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:00.930
unless you're on the witness
stand as the defendant.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.913
This is already off the rails.

00:01:02.913 --> 00:01:06.364
We're off to a cracking
good start here. (chuckles)

00:01:06.364 --> 00:01:08.700
- You can tell by looking at him.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.400
- Okay. Well, so that's
completely improper racism.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.430
If there is a jury,
somewhere in this courtroom,

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.820
that's probably immediate
grounds for a mistrial.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.050
You're not allowed to
engage in overt racism

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.360
while the trial is going on!

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.090
This is insane.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:29.010
- Order!
(gavel bangs)

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.560
Defendant, do you have
any proof of your alibi?

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.550
- Oh, now I remember Dhar Mann.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.920
I think Drew Gooden or Danny Gonzalez,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.180
or both of them did a video on this.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:39.740
They're videos where you...

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.020
It's totally unrealistic,

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.180
but you feel good at the end of the video.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.160
Now, I remember.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:49.720
- Uh, no, we don't have
any proof, your honor.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.710
- Okay. Okay.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.570
So (exhales) where are we
in the trial right now?

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.010
The judge is just flat asking the defense

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.120
if they have evidence.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.720
There's a whole trial that you go through.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.930
You call witnesses. You present evidence.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:06.470
There are motions at limine
to admit things into evidence.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.347
You don't just ask the council like,

00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:12.047
"Do you have any evidence
to support your claim here?"

00:02:13.814 --> 00:02:17.890
And why can't the defense
counsel come up with evidence

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:21.500
that this person wasn't even
in the city at the time?

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.500
- Okay, well, if there's no evidence

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.330
to corroborate Robert's story-

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.494
- That's insane!

00:02:28.494 --> 00:02:32.410
(stammering) Okay, so
you're not in Philadelphia.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:33.660
You're in Los Angeles.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.380
How did you get there?
Who did you talk to?

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:37.470
Did you buy anything?

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.950
Did you make a cell phone conversation?

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.100
You brought your cell phone, right?

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:41.933
You have a mobile phone.

00:02:41.933 --> 00:02:43.200
You could ping a cell tower.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:44.090
You probably bought something.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.310
You had to have a ticket
to get to Los Angeles.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.050
He didn't walk there.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.610
This is insanity! How...

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.760
There's no possible way
that a defense attorney

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.683
would be so incompetent as
to have zero evidence at all.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.410
- Excuse me. I must use the restroom.

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:03.900
(Lionel running)
(clock ticking)

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:05.960
- And this is the thing
that really bothers me

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:08.840
is that even if you have a witness

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.170
and it's just, you know, he-said-she-said,

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.970
that's still considered evidence.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.850
A witness testifying is
often direct evidence

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.400
and it's this, what a
lot of people consider,

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.850
the strongest you could have.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.020
My mind is reeling.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:27.840
It's absolutely a roller coaster
inside my brain right now.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.400
Boy. Wow.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:32.305
- The court finds the defendant
Robert Williams guilty.

00:03:32.305 --> 00:03:33.475
- There's (stammering)

00:03:33.475 --> 00:03:34.433
(Devin laughing)

00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:37.620
There's no jury here. It's just the judge.

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.987
He's just... (laughing)

00:03:39.987 --> 00:03:43.130
"Do you have any evidence
Mr. Defense council?

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:45.190
No? All right. Guilty."

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.450
That's not how a criminal trial works.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.283
I mean, you know this.

00:03:49.283 --> 00:03:50.116
There's a jury.

00:03:50.116 --> 00:03:52.340
You walk to the jury, you present evidence

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.170
and then you instruct the jury.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.320
(Devin laughing)

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.153
This is...

00:03:58.421 --> 00:04:03.421
I'm already broken and we're
30 seconds into this video.

00:04:03.799 --> 00:04:04.632
(Devin laughs)

00:04:04.632 --> 00:04:05.720
- No.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:09.200
No, please. Not my baby boy. (weeping)

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.980
- Okay, why are we letting
people in the gallery talk?

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:12.993
- I know he didn't!

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:15.350
- All right. Yeah, all right,
so there's an outburst.

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:19.170
- Please, you have to
believe me. I'm innocent!

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:20.763
I'd put that on my life!

00:04:22.765 --> 00:04:24.420
You know I didn't do this.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.603
You know it!
- Order!

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.142
Mr. Williams, that's enough
- I mean, does he?

00:04:28.142 --> 00:04:29.430
- not another word.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.290
- Why would the prosecutor
know he didn't do this?

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.283
The defense didn't present
any evidence whatsoever.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.330
- We'll reconvene tomorrow for sentencing.

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:40.910
- Oh, yeah. Sentencing tomorrow. Yeah.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:43.770
Courts are so backlogged, especially now.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:46.240
Sentencing wouldn't happen
for probably a few weeks

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.680
I would think.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.513
- Daddy!

00:04:48.513 --> 00:04:50.924
No, please!

00:04:50.924 --> 00:04:52.453
Don't go!

00:04:52.453 --> 00:04:54.890
- Everything's gonna be
okay, sweetie, I promise.

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:56.263
Daddy loves you so much!

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.894
- What if he's telling the truth?

00:04:58.894 --> 00:04:59.727
(Devin laughs)

00:04:59.727 --> 00:05:01.520
What if we got the wrong guy?

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.180
- Yeah, we just convicted him.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:04.410
What if he's telling the truth

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.570
based on absolutely nothing whatsoever?

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.344
- What, you don't think
I know what I'm doing?

00:05:09.344 --> 00:05:11.370
- No, no, no, I'm not
saying that, I'm just...

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:13.750
- I'm saying both of you
don't know what you're doing.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:15.220
- Wondering if maybe we
shouldn't take another look

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:16.200
at the facts.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.990
- I just won a case. (laughs)

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.710
Who cares whether he did it or not?

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:23.190
- Okay.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.760
Prosecutors are stereotyped this way,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:28.820
but that's not the
philosophy that they have.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:29.850
They want to convict people

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.520
that have actually done something.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.250
And if someone didn't do
that, then by and large,

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:36.630
every prosecutor I know

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.530
would not want to convict that person.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:42.150
Now, granted, I guess
there was a trial here

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.890
that we didn't see and
you got a conviction.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.600
So there was evidence that
this person did something

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:48.960
and presumably it was evidence
beyond a reasonable doubt,

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.170
but, you know, still this is
not the time or the place.

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:53.100
- All that's important

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.930
is I'm one step closer to a promotion.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.020
- Promotion for what? Is this guy...

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:00.790
Okay, so this guy must be an
assistant district attorney

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.520
or deputy district attorney.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.180
I mean, what promotion is he angling for?

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.710
A management position of some kind?

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.970
Oh, and also,

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:12.340
so you convicted a guy of
assault with a deadly weapon.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.740
This is as routine as it's going to get

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.640
for your sort of mid-level
felony district attorney.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.860
Like that's not going to help
you get a promotion anywhere.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.453
(chuckles) So dumb.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:29.237
- And besides people like
him, they're all the same.

00:06:29.237 --> 00:06:30.470
Just a bunch of criminals.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:31.303
- All right. Great.

00:06:31.303 --> 00:06:32.610
So we've got some overt racism.

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.063
Well, I mean, at least we
know who the bad guys are.

00:06:38.132 --> 00:06:39.810
(paper rustling)

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:40.910
- Please.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:43.230
Sir please. (weeping)

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.283
it's never too late to do the right thing.

00:06:46.283 --> 00:06:48.410
- I mean, it is sort of too late.

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.330
He was convicted here.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.200
It's really hard to undo a conviction.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:54.680
There's not a whole lot
that you can do here,

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.342
but let's see.

00:06:56.342 --> 00:06:58.759
(soft music)

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:05.177
- Yeah, well, I could
see my headlines now.

00:07:05.177 --> 00:07:10.177
"DA Graham Gives Low-Life
Criminal Life in Prison".

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.033
- Yeah.

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:13.070
Well, okay, that headline
is not going to happen

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:15.310
because he was convicted of
assault with a deadly weapon,

00:07:15.310 --> 00:07:18.280
which does not carry a
life sentence with it.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:21.300
And I think you're already
the district attorney here.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.770
Also, no newspaper is going
to run with that story

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:26.480
because this happens a
thousand times a day.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:27.910
This is... (laughing)

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.449
Like, no one's going to care about this.

00:07:30.449 --> 00:07:33.130
Oh man. So ridiculous.

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.640
- Keep up the great work.

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.019
- He's innocent!

00:07:36.019 --> 00:07:37.545
- (chuckles) He's innocent.
- He's innocent.

00:07:37.545 --> 00:07:39.459
- (sighs) Can you give us a minute?

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:40.292
- Yeah.

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:41.235
(Devin chuckles)

00:07:41.235 --> 00:07:43.277
- What a deep(beep).

00:07:43.277 --> 00:07:46.740
"He's innocent". What an ass(beep).

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.770
- What are you talking about?

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.180
- He wasn't in Philly when it happened.

00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:51.150
His story checks out.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:53.250
Look, I have his credit
card statements right here.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.010
He was making purchases in Los Angeles

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:57.066
while the crime was happening.

00:07:57.066 --> 00:07:57.990
- (laughs) What do you mean

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.793
you have his credit card statements?

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.530
Okay. Okay. All right.

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:05.630
If the district attorney had this evidence

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.680
and didn't turn it over to the defense,

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.080
that is a Brady Violation
left, right, and center.

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:14.280
That is ridiculous that they
wouldn't have turned that over

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.590
to the defense.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.240
Presumably, they had to subpoena
the credit card company.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:19.240
So they went through all the rigamarole

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.170
of getting this evidence.

00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:23.170
If the prosecutor has
exculpatory evidence,

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:25.620
you have to turn that over to the defense.

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:28.730
So presumably, he had all of
this stuff in front of him.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:31.270
He just didn't turn it
over to the defense.

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:33.660
So this is actually one of
those rare circumstances

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:36.150
where the defense would be probably able

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:37.360
to overturn this conviction.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.047
So once the defense gets this evidence,

00:08:39.047 --> 00:08:41.640
the defense would then file
a motion with the judge

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.440
to vacate the judgment

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:46.110
and to dismiss the
judgment with prejudice.

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.880
And then maybe you ask the
court to sanction the prosecutor

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.850
for very clear miscarriage of justice.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:53.020
But, yeah, okay.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.170
So that the prosecutors had this

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.370
from the very beginning is insanity.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.070
- We have to get these to
the defense right away.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:01.523
- Have you lost your mind?

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:07.600
- I mean, uh, maybe
somebody stole his card.

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.255
Maybe he gave it to him.

00:09:09.255 --> 00:09:10.090
(Devin scoffs)

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:11.270
- Yeah. So it doesn't matter.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:12.900
The prosecutor doesn't get to come up

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.250
with alternative explanations.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:15.760
If the evidence is exculpatory,

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.230
you have to turn it over to the defense.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:20.310
- I can think of a thousand reasons.

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.999
- Yeah, it doesn't
matter how many reasons.

00:09:21.999 --> 00:09:23.332
- That's enough.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.377
The case is over.

00:09:27.377 --> 00:09:29.841
(paper tearing)
(somber music)

00:09:29.841 --> 00:09:31.362
Do you hear me?

00:09:31.362 --> 00:09:34.640
(coffee pouring)

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:35.473
- I have proof.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:37.980
- What is it?

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.270
- You already had proof.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:42.670
- I contacted a local convenience store

00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:44.130
and I found a photo of him.

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:45.530
Take a look.

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.220
He couldn't have committed
a crime in Philly

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.560
if he was buying a hot dog in LA.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.270
- Okay. So this is a little bit different.

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:53.730
This is newly acquired evidence.

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:55.370
So if the defense found this evidence

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:56.450
and they wanted to admit it

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:58.160
and overturn the initial conviction,

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.110
they would have had to have
filed a motion for new trial.

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:03.280
And the standard there is that
they wouldn't have been able

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.360
to discover this evidence
with reasonable diligence

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.920
before the trial itself.

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.410
But this probably wasn't
that hard to find.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:14.320
They probably wouldn't have
been able to meet that standard

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:15.920
because this defense attorney

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:17.180
just completely dropped the ball

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:19.640
and said there was absolutely
no evidence at all.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.270
However, since the prosecution now

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:22.870
has it in their possession,

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:24.580
it's probably still a Brady violation

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:26.350
that they're not turning it over.

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:27.890
But still, they didn't have the obligation

00:10:27.890 --> 00:10:29.510
to affirmatively go out
and find this evidence

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:30.343
in the first place.

00:10:30.343 --> 00:10:31.350
That's up to the defense.

00:10:31.350 --> 00:10:32.713
And they dropped the ball.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:35.030
- Doesn't even look like him.

00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:37.690
- It's him. I had facial
recognition confirm it.

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.713
- What? What facial recognition?

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:42.693
This isn't an episode of "CSI".

00:10:44.142 --> 00:10:47.142
(keyboard clacking)

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.650
- Has anyone else seen this?

